    "q-182004": {
      "dom_id": "step-q182004",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following is a representation of the set $S=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]$ in the Cartesian plane?",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=\\{-2,1,2\\}\\times [0,2]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is either $-2,1,$ or $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in \\{-2,1,2\\},\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/449b948df4a32c540281ddac1659c261e2908433ecc1f383b26cb5cb4aa29756.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/922c96e62e8ed3481e595595e060956fbaf191cd3ec7636e7a874b11fea3bcd5.png)\n\nNow, consider ${S}_{3}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,2].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,2]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis set is represented by the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/0d3cb62f7e9fb47823d0a2ba8c071e09adbea673ad54a5d1b014e8882aeff472.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\nS={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $S$ can be represented by the three line segments shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242415/assets/f7cecda03e84263537cb29bbd14bb4dccc2e60fada10db1ca96798490e978676.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
